==Icelandic==
{{wikipedia|hungur|lang=is}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈsvauŋkʏr/}}
* {{rhymes|is|auŋkʏr|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|is|non|svangr||slender, slim, thin}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*swangaz||thin, slim}}. The original meaning in Germanic was probably 'curved inwards', discernible from the derived Old Norse {{m|non|svangi||flank, groin}}, i.e., 'the sides of the abdomen which curve inwards slightly'. Within Icelandic the meaning developed from 'thin' to 'feeling hungry'.<ref>{{R:gem:EWGP|head=swanga-|page=572}}</ref> Cognates are {{cog|fo|svangur||hungry}}, {{cog|da|svang||thin, famished}}, {{cog|no|svang||hungry}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|svånger||thin, hungry, empty}}, Middle {{cog|sv|svanger||slender, emaciated, very thin}}, {{cog|gmh|swanger||flexible, slender}}.

Further origin unsure, perhaps related to Old Irish {{m|sga|seng||narrow, thin}}. Not related to Danish {{m|da|svanger}}, Norwegian {{m|no|svanger}}, Dutch {{m|nl|zwanger}}, and German {{m|de|schwanger||pregnant}}.

